WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.728
Three graders for one task.

2
00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:07.896
For most agent tasks, use three kinds of grader together, because each misses something the others catch.

3
00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:10.395
End-state checks (code) read the outcome.

4
00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:13.469
Did exactly one post land in #proj-status?

5
00:00:13.749 --> 00:00:18.464
Does it mention every issue that changed state this week, and no closed issue as open?

6
00:00:18.744 --> 00:00:20.301
Were no other effects produced?

7
00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:24.699
These are cheap, deterministic, and closest to what users care about.

8
00:00:24.979 --> 00:00:31.379
Trajectory assertions (code over the transcript) check rules about the path that matter regardless of outcome.

9
00:00:31.659 --> 00:00:39.765
tau-bench’s authors note that a correct final database can hide a policy violation, such as acting without the user’s confirmation.

10
00:00:40.045 --> 00:00:55.448
For your agent: no write tool executed without an approval record; no outbound call after private data entered the context unless approved; at most N tool calls; no tool called with arguments that failed validation more than twice.

11
00:00:55.728 --> 00:00:59.184
Studio 3’s exercise built this kind of check; reuse it.

12
00:00:59.464 --> 00:01:09.875
Output quality (a model-based grader, or a person) judges what code cannot: is the status report accurate, clear, appropriately prioritized for a stakeholder?

13
00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:20.523
Use a rubric with binary criteria, and calibrate the judge against your own labels exactly as in Studio 3, lessons 5 and 6, before you trust its numbers.

14
00:01:20.803 --> 00:01:22.573
Then decide how to combine them.

15
00:01:22.853 --> 00:01:28.272
Anthropic’s guide lists weighted scores, binary (every grader must pass), or a hybrid.

16
00:01:28.552 --> 00:01:39.027
For agents, a hybrid works well: safety and policy assertions are hard requirements, end-state checks define success, and quality scores are reported alongside.

17
00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:41.013
Finally, grade the grader.

18
00:01:41.293 --> 00:02:01.155
Zhu and colleagues’ 2025 paper on rigorous agentic benchmarks found task-setup and reward-design flaws in well-known benchmarks, including one that counted empty responses as successful, and estimated that such issues can misstate performance by up to 100% in relative terms.

19
00:02:01.435 --> 00:02:03.227
Your eval can have the same bugs.

20
00:02:03.507 --> 00:02:09.480
Include a scenario where doing nothing must fail, and one where a deliberately wrong report must fail.
